"""Content C: Norse Wheel of the Year and Plants & Trees."""

CH5 = [
("lore", "Norse Wheel of the Year", "An honest wheel",
  ["The old northern year was measured by work. In autumn, hay was stacked, cattle came in from the pasture, meat was salted for winter, and boats were hauled up onto land. In spring, lambs were born and seed went into the ground. Five seasonal turnings survive by name: Winter Nights, Yule, Dísablót (Disablot), Sigrblót (Sigrblot), and Midsummer. They come to us through Icelandic sagas, Snorri Sturluson’s Heimskringla, Christian calendars, and folk memory. The sources are thin in places, and customs varied from district to district.",
  "This book stays close to northern sources. You may know the eight-spoke wheel, which spaces the solstices and equinoxes evenly. It was put together in the last century from several cultures, so it is a modern tool and not an old northern one. This book uses the five northern turnings instead, and it marks thin evidence as thin. Midsummer is a later folk custom. The book watches the moon for pleasure and does not force a pattern onto it.",
  "A year like this is learned by doing. One marked date teaches more than any diagram, and one small task you finish is worth more than a shelf of plans. Start with one date. Write it down in ink, and the rest of the year will have a fixed point to measure by.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The five turnings are named in Icelandic sagas, Snorri’s Heimskringla, and folk custom, but the sources are thin. The eight-spoke wheel is a modern invention."],
  "Find which of the five turnings comes next. Mark its date in ink and read its pages once, with a warm drink in hand. Choose one small task to finish before the week ends and write it beside the date. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Norse Wheel of the Year", "How each turning works here",
  ["Most turnings follow the same pattern. First comes the story, as the Poetic Edda and Icelandic sagas tell it. Next comes the old practice, as far as it can be recovered. Then comes a practice built on it, sized for a flat and one evening, then something for the table, and last ruled pages for your own writing. Dísablót has only one page, because the sources say almost nothing about it.",
  "Old winters ran on food, fire, and company. In these pages you will meet the Yule peace, toasts to the ancestors, cleaned thresholds, and hill walks. Some of it is old, some is custom that lasted into recent times, and some was written for this book. Each page says which.",
  "Your household already knows how to feast. Birthday bread comes to the table on its own plate. One soup fixes everything. One pudding is always asked for. This chapter adds five old names to the dishes you already love. Say an old name over a dish you already cook, and the meal becomes a marker of the season.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> How this chapter works. Its method is modern, built on old sources."],
  "Sit down for twenty minutes and list the dishes that already mean joy at your table. Copy that list onto the reflection pages when you reach them. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Norse Wheel of the Year", "The Rök stone sidebar",
  ["A tall grey stone stands in Östergötland, in Sweden. It is covered edge to edge with runes. It is the Rök stone, raised in the 800s. It holds the longest runic text from the Viking Age known to us. Its lines use alliteration, the repeated starting sounds of poetry, and tell of heroes, battles, a wolf, and robbery. The stone was carved with great care. Weather has worn it, but its writing can still be read.",
   "The stone is a memorial and nothing more. Scholars still disagree about many of its lines. Nothing on it lists a festival or a calendar date, so it cannot prove any of the turnings here. Later calendar sticks, carved wooden sticks that counted feasts and moons, are a separate source. Their marks differ from valley to valley. The turnings do not depend on the stone.",
   "The old calendar sticks share one habit worth borrowing. They keep count over time. The moon still waxes and wanes over your street. Looking at the sky twice a month builds steady attention. Short notes kept over time are more useful than arguments about the past. Step outside, look up, and write down what you see.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Rök stone dates to the 800s, and calendar sticks come later. No one knows whether the stone had any festival use."],
  "Step outside tonight and find the moon. Note whether it waxes or wanes and write one line about the sky over your street. Keep those notes across the two months of the tracker. Give it four dated lines."),
("tracker", "Norse Wheel of the Year", "Moon tracker, two months: watch the sky, don’t rebuild old rites. Note new and full moon dates.",
  ["Month one, new moon — date + sky",
   "Month one, full moon — date + sky",
   "Month one — what changed around you?",
   "Month two, new moon — date + sky",
   "Month two, full moon — date + sky",
   "Month two — what changed around you?",
   "Which turning falls inside these two moons?",
   "One line: what did the moon teach?"]),
("lore", "Winter Nights", "Vetrnætr (Vetrnaetr): kin and burial mounds",
  ["Winter Nights (Vetrnætr) is the autumn feast that opens the winter half of the year, in mid-October. The harvest is in, the herds are housed, and the autumn slaughter is done. Icelandic sagas and calendars name it. Families gathered to honor their kin and the land spirits, and to thank the dísir (disir), female guardian spirits linked to households. A blót, a gift to gods and spirits often shared as a meal, was held, and toasts, drinks raised to a name or a wish, were drunk. Some sagas show toasts to Njörðr and Freyr for peace and plenty. Burial mounds were visited as the leaves turned.",
  "The feast had two jobs at once. The living kin were fed first. Then the dead of the burial mounds and the dísir were thanked. The threshold was swept clean, and the main work of the day was to bring everyone, living and dead, under one roof.",
  "The old feast turned toward the dead. If there is a grave or an old mound near you, visit it as the leaves turn, and say one name aloud.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Winter Nights blot is in Icelandic sagas and calendars, but thinly. The details come from later folk custom."],
  "Visit a grave, a mound, or a place where someone is remembered. Say one name aloud. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Winter Nights", "Vetrnætr: table and kin",
  ["The feast uses food stored for winter. Salt pork comes with mashed root vegetables. Morning porridge comes with butter. Dark bread comes with thick slices of cheese. Ale or a fruit cordial is raised in toasts. Old feasts used the food from the slaughter season. Eat in the warmest room you have. Light a few candles against the October dark.",
   "When the meal ends, the toasts begin. A toast is a drink raised to a name or a wish. Keep all the toasts to ten minutes. Raise three toasts aloud in plain words. Toast the kin present, then thank the dísir and landvættir (land spirits). Toast a winter kept well. Finish each cup. The food is folk survival. The spoken toasts keep an old custom alive today.",
   "Three short sentences, kept for a season, teach more about family than a long list of resolutions. Keep each toast plain enough for a child to understand. Speak it standing, with your feet on the floor. Then keep saying it through the winter. Plain thanks spoken aloud link the living and the dead in your family’s memory.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Slaughter-season foods and toasts come from later folk custom, with hints in the sagas."],
  "Rough your three toasts out on the reflection pages first, where crossings out cost nothing. Trim until a child could understand, then speak them standing at the table with feet on the floor. Toast the kin present, then thank the dísir and landvættir. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Yule", "Yule: midwinter peace",
  ["Yule is the old northern midwinter feast, held in the longest dark of the year, and doors were often barred early. It was a time of oaths sworn over a boar or a ring, which bound people to a promise. It brought toasts to gods and ancestors, and a peace over households, so that even enemies could eat without feud. Some Poetic Edda poems and Icelandic sagas tell of vows made at Yule being kept later in battle. Slaughter was done and stores were full.",
  "Folk custom keeps the same shape in plainer clothes. Straw, a candle, and a cleaned house mark the season. The home grows quiet as the old names are spoken. You do not need a crowd or costumes.",
  "Yule is the time for vows. Before you make a new one, look back at the vows you made last winter and say honestly which you kept. Then speak the names of the dead over supper, one sentence each.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Yule feasting and oaths are thinly recorded in the Poetic Edda and Icelandic sagas. The details come from later folk custom."],
  "Sit still for one evening with last year’s vows. Note which you kept and which you did not. Then speak the names of your dead over supper, one sentence each. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Yule", "Yule: the vow and the supper",
  ["The old practice centered on feast and vow. People gathered and a peace was announced, and feuds paused under it. Oaths were sworn over a boar, a vow made in front of witnesses and kept later, even in battle. The hall was crowded with meat stored for winter, and doors were barred early against the cold.",
  "Keep the shape at your own table. Make a plain supper from stored food: a stew, bread, apples and nuts. Before the meal, each person speaks one vow aloud for the year, with the others as witnesses. Set one extra plate for the dead, a custom kept in Scandinavian homes into recent times, and speak each ancestor’s name with thanks.",
  "Write the vows down so they can be checked at the next Yule. The Ancestors pages give a fuller way to remember the dead. The next morning, leave a share out for the birds.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Feast and vow-drinking are thinly recorded in the sagas. The extra plate comes from later Scandinavian folk custom."],
  "Hold this supper on one evening this week. Cook one simple dish, lay the extra plate, and speak each ancestor’s name. Then speak one vow for the year before a witness. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Dísablót", "Dísablót: the ladies’ feast",
  ["Dísablót is a late-winter feast that the Icelandic sagas name but never describe in detail. The name means an offering to the dísir (disir), female guardian spirits linked to kin, households, and fate. Some sources link Freyja to them, since she is also called Vanadís, lady of the Vanir. Only the name survives in the sagas. A later Swedish market, Disting, is sometimes linked to this feast, and the Idisi in the Merseburg charms were women called on to help warriors.",
  "The sources are thin, and this page says so. No prayer, altar plan, or menu survives, and the Disting fair is a late link, not proof. This book builds one honest practice, clearly modern: a late winter supper that honors the women whose work shaped your household, living and dead, by name and aloud. Naming them gives them a place at the table.",
  "Once a year is enough. Cook one dish from stored food, and name each woman in one true sentence while you eat. The fuller supper plan is on the Ancestors pages. Naming women who are often forgotten keeps their memory alive in your family.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The dísir and the dísablót are named in the Icelandic sagas, but thinly. Disting is a later market. The supper was written for this book."],
  "Hold your supper on one February evening, in under an hour. Cook one dish from stored food and speak each woman’s name with one true sentence. Log the names and sentences beside your ancestor cards. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Sigrblót", "Sigrblót: turn to summer",
  ["Sigrblót (Sigrblot) means “victory sacrifice,” an offering made at the start of summer for success. The kings’ sagas, such as Ynglinga saga in Snorri’s Heimskringla, name it as a spring offering. It was made to ask for fair winds and success in war and at sea, and toasts were drunk before ships set out. Picture tar on rope at the shore, gulls over the crews, and a sail filling in an April wind.",
  "Crews mended their gear before they sailed. Your household can keep the same shape: name the work you are about to begin, and say aloud what would make it a success. The name Sigrblót is recorded only thinly, and the household version is modern.",
  "The old rite was a toast and a vow for the season’s work. Name one voyage you will launch this summer, such as a project, a trip, or a change, and say it aloud before a witness.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Sigrblót is named in the kings’ sagas, such as Ynglinga saga, but thinly. The household version was rebuilt for this book."],
  "Name the voyage you will launch this summer and what would make it a success. Say it aloud to one witness. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Sigrblót", "Sigrblót: table and pledge",
  ["The table holds the first fresh foods of the year, which feel like a sharp break from winter. Green salads come with fresh cheese. Oatcakes come with honey. Home-brewed ale is poured where custom allows. Grill something outdoors if you have the space. People in flats can skip the grill and share warm bread. Set the meal near safe light, such as lamps or a few candles well away from curtains. The meal may run later than usual.",
   "A pledge is a promise made out loud. You can make it alone or with dozens of people. Name one voyage you will launch this summer, such as a project, a trip, or a change. Say its course, its crew, and the harbor you are heading for. Drink the cup in front of witnesses, however few. Shared greens and shared meals are folk survival. Speak it plainly and keep your word.",
   "This practice works best when you plan first. Saying a plan aloud to others makes it harder to forget. Prepare for your voyage, then keep your word, even when it is hard. Take one step you have planned, and your summer begins.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Spring greens and shared eating come from later folk custom."],
  "Write down one voyage you will launch this summer before the meal begins. Name its course, its crew, and where it should end on the reflection page. Then stand and drink to it in front of witnesses, however few. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Midsummer", "Midsummer: light that does not leave",
  ["Midsummer is a summer gathering around a bonfire and fresh green branches, and people in the north still remember it. It comes with flower crowns and hill fires. Many northern farms kept some part of it. Folk collectors such as Tillhagen recorded Midsummer greens and the custom of picking herbs at noon, and fires lit against a night that barely gets dark. Crowns wilted by midnight.",
  "Joy taken with care is the mark of this day. People climb hills, enjoy the greenery only where that is allowed, and leave no trace. Food from many hands is shared. Name the grower, the carrier, and the cook. A flower means more when you name the people who brought it. Named thanks last longer than the feast.",
  "Share one joy with one witness before the week ends. That is Midsummer kept small. A swim, a song, or a nap in full daylight will do. Choose one joy and tell someone about it. When you name the people who helped you find that joy, the light of the season stays with you longer.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Midsummer greens and fires come from later folk custom. No old poem is claimed for them."],
  "Name one joy you will mark at this height of summer, such as a swim, a song, or a nap in full daylight. Show it to one witness, and write how you will share it before the week ends. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Midsummer", "Midsummer: old practice, modern rite",
  ["As folk collectors recorded it, the old practice meant a fire and green plants. People walked uphill in crowds. Crowns were twisted at noon. Herbs were picked and hung up to dry. The summer light was praised where everyone could see it. Your modern practice keeps both at a size your week can hold. The hill can be a park slope, or a flight of stairs you count honestly. Your legs will feel the climb.",
   "Taste one summer food on purpose. It may be grown at home, bought, or given by a neighbor with plenty. Then give thanks out loud. Speak one promise of care to someone who can hear it. Keep the promise short and specific. Say it slowly, then say it again so it sticks. Name the food and the person who shared it, so your thanks has a clear face.",
   "In the old custom, greens and thanks belonged together. Climb a hill, speak a promise, and keep both in mind. Pick one clear step to take after the walk, and write it down.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Hill fires and crowns come from later folk custom. The home version was written for this book."],
  "Plan your walk tonight so it fits inside an hour. Pick the hill, or the stairs, and write three simple rules: what counts as the climb, how you mark it, and when it ends. Walk it this week and log it honestly. Give it three dated lines."),
]

CH6 = [
("lore", "Plants", "Safety box — read first",
  ["Some plants in this chapter can harm you, so read this page first. Yew is toxic in every part. Many herbs in the old medical books are strong, and some are dangerous in pregnancy or alongside modern medicines. Never swallow or apply anything from these pages just because an old book says so.",
  "The rule for this chapter is simple. The old remedies here are history. Look at plants, learn their names, and keep notes. Do not gather wild plants without an expert beside you, and never drink wild water. Leave no trace outdoors, and carry out everything you carry in.",
  "Check any herb against your medicines with a clinician first. If you are or may be pregnant, check with a clinician before using any herb. Careful watching and a written rule help you learn without harm.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Toxicity and caution come from classical and modern medical knowledge."],
  "Write your own safety line here, dated. Name what you will never swallow and who your expert will be. Give it one firm line."),
("lore", "Plants of the North", "Healing plants in the sagas and poems",
  ["No list of Viking Age herbs survives. What we have are scattered mentions. In Ólafs saga helga, part of Snorri’s Heimskringla, a wounded man named Þormóðr (Thormod) is offered a hot broth of leeks, onions, and other herbs by a healer woman, who wants to smell the wound to learn whether it has pierced the belly. He refuses it.",
  "The Sigrdrífumál says a cup must be blessed and guarded against danger, and that garlic must be cast into the liquor: “then I know thou wilt never have mead with treachery mingled.” (Stanza 8, translated by Benjamin Thorpe, 1866.) The Old Norse word is laukr, which means leek, onion, or garlic. The same poem teaches healers to cut runes on the bark and leaves of trees (stanza 11).",
  "The herb lists that do survive are in Icelandic medical books from the 1200s to the 1400s. The next pages cover what they say. They were written by Christian scribes, so they are medieval, not Viking Age.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Ólafs saga helga in Snorri’s Heimskringla, and the Sigrdrífumál in the Poetic Edda in Benjamin Thorpe’s 1866 translation. The medical books are described in Luthien Cangemi’s The Art of Healing in Medieval Iceland (UCL, 2024)."],
  "Look up laukr, which means leek, onion, or garlic. Name where you could find it in your own kitchen, and what the old sources used it for. Give it two dated lines."),
("lore", "Plants of the North", "Yarrow: greni",
  ["Yarrow is easy to recognize once you learn its signs. Its leaves are feathery, like green lace. Its flat white flower heads stand along hedges all summer. Old books call it soldier’s woundwort. In Old Icelandic medical books it is greni, also written millefolium, “thousand leaves.”",
  "A fifteenth-century Icelandic medical manuscript, AM 434 a 12mo, gives yarrow three uses: chew the root while fasting for toothache; drink the juice with wine to clear the urine; and crush it and lay it on a wound, which it says heals every wound remarkably well. The same manuscript also uses yarrow in a ritual to find a thief, mixing the herb with a carved sign, a spoken charm, and a Christian closing formula. These are quoted here as history, not as instructions.",
  "This book asks you only to look. Yarrow can cause skin irritation in some people. Never use it in place of prescribed medicine, and never for deep or doubtful wounds. Count ten clumps on a walk and sketch one leaf.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Yarrow as greni is in AM 434 a 12mo, as described in Luthien Cangemi’s thesis. The manuscript was edited by Kristian Kålund and translated by Ben Waggoner."],
  "On your next walk, count every yarrow clump you pass and note where the ground is damp or trodden. Sketch one leaf in five plain lines. Count ten clumps. Do not gather anything. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Plants of the North", "More herbs in the leechbooks",
  ["The same manuscript names other herbs, most of them known across medieval Europe. Betony (betonica), drunk with water at dawn, brightens the eyes, and ground in old wine and oil it drives out poison. Centaury juice mixed with honey takes the film from the eyes. Rue, grown at home, is used with honey or sour wine for running or dim eyes, and ground in oil for headache.",
  "Pennyroyal is boiled in oil and rubbed on the forehead and temples for headache, or boiled in wine or ale for a swollen belly. Horehound is said to cleanse the lungs when drunk often. Many of these match the Old English Herbarium and the Lacnunga from the tenth century, which shows that Icelandic scribes were copying a shared European tradition and giving some plants Icelandic names.",
  "So these are medieval Icelandic remedies, not Viking Age ones, and some of the plants were grown in gardens, not found wild. They are recorded here as history. This book does not recommend any of them. If you are unwell, see a clinician.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> AM 434 a 12mo and Dublin, Royal Irish Academy MS 23 D 43, as described in Luthien Cangemi’s thesis. Compare the Old English Herbarium and the Lacnunga."],
  "Which of these herbs have you seen growing near you? Name it and where. Do not pick it. Give it two dated lines."),
("lore", "Plants of the North", "Pine: tar and light",
  ["Pine was one of the most useful trees in the north. Its wood gave ship timbers and masts, and its resin was boiled into tar for sealing boats and rope. Resin-rich pine wood, called torchwood, was split for light. Knotty roots were split for kindling, and that kindling carried light through the Yule dark. In a pine stand at noon, the scent is thick. Light falls in shafts between trunks.",
   "Pine is handled today as a source of light and warmth, mostly in small amounts you buy. Thank the wood as you use it, for its resin and its kindling. Buy firewood or kindling honestly instead of cutting trees yourself. Write the date you bought or used it in the same notebook. Dry kindling you buy is more useful than big plans about forests. Use only what you have bought or been given.",
   "A tree you visit each year becomes a fixed point, something steady to measure the seasons by. Dry kindling you buy can light your midwinter nights. Visit one pine, note its date and how it stands, and it becomes a steady marker for your year.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Pine tar for ships and torchwood for light are known from archaeology and folk life."],
  "Find your nearest pine this week. Record where it grows and how it stands with date, then return at midwinter dark and add one line about light. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Plants of the North", "Plantain: læknnesgras",
  ["Plantain grows flat where feet go. Its ribbed leaves press close to the path. Its flower spikes rise like thin green candles. The Icelandic medical manuscripts translate its Latin name into plain Icelandic, læknnesgras, roughly “healing herb.”",
  "AM 434 a 12mo says to grind the root in cold water and give it to a man or a cow for fever, and promises quick recovery. English folk names for it, waybread and wound leaf, show how widely it was known. In hard years its seeds were saved and added to porridge. It stays low, gets stepped on, and still grows back.",
  "This book does not ask you to use plantain on your body or in food. The old uses are history, not instructions. Never use it in place of prescribed care, and never on deep or dirty wounds.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Plantain as læknnesgras is in AM 434 a 12mo, as described in Luthien Cangemi’s thesis. The English names and the seed use come from folk custom."],
  "Find a plantain on a path and study its ribbed leaves and spikes for ten minutes. Sketch it in five lines, and write the clinician caution twice beside your log. Do not pick or use any part of it. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Trees", "Ash: the world tree",
  ["In Grímnismál (Grimnir’s sayings), the ash tree Yggdrasill holds up the nine worlds. You can see why when you stand beneath a large one. Its bark is grey and ridged. Its buds are black. Its winged seeds hang in bunches. Its timber is springy and strong, so it was used for spears and tool handles. In the Poetic Edda, Yggdrasill is an ash, and its roots reach to wells where the Nornir (the Norns, who shape fate) sit. Put your palm on the bark. It feels cool and straight.",
  "This tree grows straight. Year after year it adds steady wood, the kind once used for spears and shafts.",
  "Visit one ash in each of the four seasons, and it becomes a familiar friend. Watch the buds open and the birds change above the bark. Walking with one tree through the year gives you a steady place to measure your own growth.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The ash as world-tree is in the Poetic Edda, and ash timber was widely used."],
  "Visit an ash tree this week for twenty minutes, and write where it stands as your first entry. Then log it across all four seasons. Give it four dated lines."),
("lore", "Trees", "Birch: bright beginner",
  ["Birch leads the spring, with white bark and bright green leaves. It is among the first trees to leaf and among the first to turn gold. Northern farms used its bark for containers and roofing. Coppice workers (people who cut young shoots for reuse) cut birch rods for baskets. One small tree serves many uses. Peel only bark that has already fallen. Never cut a ring around a living trunk, which can kill the tree.",
  "Look closely and you will see why people valued it: light, flexible wood, waterproof bark, and early leaves.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Birch use is known from archaeology and folk life. The birch stanza is in the Anglo-Saxon rune poem."],
  "Find a birch this week. Note its bark, buds, and leaves, and the date. Return in a month and note what changed. Give it two dated lines."),
("lore", "Trees", "Rowan at the threshold",
  ["Rowan trees grow near doorways across northern countries. People planted them beside houses and holy wells. Old custom trusted them on doorways, gateposts, and cradles, believing they guarded against harm. It is a small tree with a light crown. Its berries stand scarlet against grey stone. People valued it as a guard more than as timber. Look for rowans near doors as you walk.",
  "In Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, the rowan is called the salvation of Þórr, because Þórr once saved himself from a flooded river by grabbing a rowan on the bank. You can care for a rowan in plain ways: notice it often, take only fallen wood, and never cut a branch without permission.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Rowan at the threshold is folk custom. The rowan as Þórr’s salvation is in Snorri’s Prose Edda."],
  "Find a rowan near a doorway on a walk. Note the date, the berries, and where it stands. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Trees", "Yew: the keeper of flame",
  ["The yew can outlive the trees around it. It stands dark green through every month, and every part of it is poisonous. In the Grímnismál, Ydalir, “Yew-dales,” is the home of Ullr, the archer god, and yew was prized for bows. The Anglo-Saxon rune poem calls the yew “on the outside a rough tree, hard and fast in the earth, a guardian of flame and a joy upon an estate.”",
  "Take no teas, no berries, and no experiments. Look, learn the name, and touch nothing. Take nothing away from a yew except notes.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Ydalir is in the Grímnismál in the Poetic Edda. The yew stanza is in the Anglo-Saxon rune poem. The poison is a plain fact."],
  "Find a yew near you, or a photograph of one. Note the date and what you see. Do not touch it. Give it one dated line."),
("lore", "Trees", "Oak: the slow king",
  ["Oak measures time in centuries. You feel it when you stand under one. Its timber built ships and door posts in the north. Oaks can stand for hundreds of years, even with hollow trunks. In old stories, the oak is a central, steady tree. Put your palm on the bark. It feels ridged, cold, and steady. A lone oak in a field often stands for generations. Stand close and feel its size.",
   "The oak takes its time. Season after season it adds slow, strong wood. An old oak can keep standing with a hollow trunk. This tree teaches you to think in seasons, not days.",
   "Watching an oak through the seasons keeps you steady. Guess its girth (width around the trunk), and note the birds, year after year. One oak followed through all four seasons gives your year a steady point to return to.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Viking ships such as Oseberg and Gokstad were built mostly of oak. The oak stanza is in the Anglo-Saxon rune poem."],
  "Choose one oak near you and walk to it this week, taking about twenty minutes. Note where it stands and how it looks today, including its lean, its branches, and the light. Then log it across all four seasons. Give it four dated lines."),
]
